{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Do not look behind you, and do not stand in all the plain</b> — the measurement of the verse is: do not stand in all the plain, and do not look behind you after you have escaped, for until Lot reaches the mountain, no evil will descend upon them. And for this reason he said, <b>but I cannot flee to the mountain, lest the evil overtake me</b> while I am in the plain, for You will not delay for me even a little, as You said: \"Flee quickly.\" And Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 19:17) said: \"Do not look behind you\" — you and all that is yours. And so: \"You shall not eat of it\" (i.e., the singular address includes his household). But what need is there for this? The punishment here is not because they transgress the angel's warning, but rather he warned them of his own knowledge that punishment would come upon them through that act of looking, and he warned Lot for his merit, and whoever hears and is careful saves his own life. And the matter of the prohibition against looking — Rashi said: you joined in their wickedness, and by the merit of Abraham you are saved; you are not entitled to see their punishment. And there is another matter in it, for seeing the air of pestilence and all contagious diseases causes great harm and transmits them, and so does thinking about them. Therefore the afflicted man with tzara'at must be shut away and dwell alone (Leviticus 13:46), and likewise those bitten by rabid animals — such as a rabid dog and others — when they see water and in every reflection they perceive the image of the harmful agent and go mad and die, as they said in tractate Yoma (Yoma ch. 4), and the men of nature have mentioned this. Therefore Lot's wife became a pillar of salt, for the plague came upon her in her thought as she saw the sulfur and salt descending upon them from the heavens, and it adhered to her. And I am inclined to say that when YHWH destroyed these cities, the destroying angel was standing between the earth and the heavens, visible in a flame of fire, like the matter of the destroying angel that David saw (I Chronicles 21:16), and therefore he forbade them to look. And in Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer (ch. 25) they said in accordance with this matter: do not look behind you, for the Shekhinah of the Holy One, blessed be He, descended to rain upon Sodom and upon Gomorrah sulfur and fire. Irit, the wife of Lot — her compassion was stirred for her married daughters in Sodom, and she looked behind her to see whether they were coming after her, and she saw behind the Shekhinah, and became a pillar of salt."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "Ramban cites Ibn Ezra's Genesis reference as Genesis 19:17, which I have rendered directly. The name 'Irit' for Lot's wife is preserved as it appears in Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer; 'men of nature' translates אנשי הטבע, likely referring to natural philosophers or physicians."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:32:06.448749215Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}